---
description: Menu bar Mac app that saves a project's windows as a named room and brings them back, laid out, from a ⌥Space palette.
---

***definitions***

- :RoomsApp: is a macOS menu bar application with no Dock icon.

- :CurrentScreen: is the screen the mouse pointer is on; its visible area is the screen minus the menu bar and the Dock.

- :MenuBarItem: is the status item of :RoomsApp: in the macOS menu bar, with a drop-down menu.

- :Palette: is a floating panel of :RoomsApp: opened with the global hotkey ⌥Space, with a text field for typing and a list of results below it.

- :PaletteDesign: is the visual design of :Palette:.
  - It is described in [resources/palette-design.md](resources/palette-design.md).

- :WindowPicker: is a view of :RoomsApp: that shows all open windows as cards, one card per window, for choosing which windows belong together.

- :GettingStarted: is a view of :RoomsApp: that shows the setup steps, opened from :MenuBarItem:.

- :AppWindow: is a window of a running application on the Mac, identified by its application and its window identity so it can be found again while the application runs.
  - Application name and icon (required)
  - Window title (required, may be empty)
  - Frame: position and size on screen (required)
  - Minimum size: the smallest width and height the application allows for this window, measured when the room is saved (required)

- :Layout: is a named arrangement of the :AppWindow: items of a room on one screen, one of Auto, Focus, Columns, Grid, or Stack.
  - Auto: resolved to Focus, Columns, Grid, or Stack each time the room is shown
  - Focus: the main window fills the left two thirds of the screen; the other windows share the right third, stacked top to bottom
  - Columns: all windows side by side, equal widths, full height
  - Grid: windows in a grid of equal cells, rows and columns as square as the count allows, filled left to right, top to bottom
  - Stack: windows cascaded so each title bar peeks out below the previous one; chosen only when nothing else fits

- :LayoutEngine: is the component of :RoomsApp: that computes a frame for each :AppWindow: of an ordered list from a :Layout: and the visible area of :CurrentScreen:.

- :LayoutPreviewDesign: is the visual design of the layout preview overlay.
  - It is described in [resources/layout-preview-design.md](resources/layout-preview-design.md).

- :Notification: is a short message of :RoomsApp: shown briefly above everything on :CurrentScreen:, such as "Open <application>, then open “<room name>” again".
  - Message: the text (required)
  - Applications: the bundle identifiers and names of the applications the message is about, in order (optional)

- :NotificationDesign: is the visual design of :Notification:.
  - It is described in [resources/notification-design.md](resources/notification-design.md).

- :Room: is a named set of :AppWindow: items with the :Layout: they are shown in.
  - Name (required, unique among rooms, case-insensitive)
  - Windows: ordered list of :AppWindow: items; position 1 is the main window (required, at least one)
  - Layout: the :Layout: the room remembers (required)
  - Direct key: a number 1 to 9, unique among rooms; a new :Room: gets the lowest free number, and only a :Room: beyond the ninth has none (optional)
  - Last shown: the time the room was last shown (optional)

- :WindowCatalog: is the component of :RoomsApp: that lists the open :AppWindow: items of all running applications, and finds a saved :AppWindow: again among them.

- :RoomList: is the collection of all :Room: items, kept across launches of :RoomsApp:. Initially empty.

- :CurrentRoom: is the :Room: most recently shown by :RoomsApp:, or none. It changes only when a :Room: is shown or saved with ⌘S, and clears when that :Room: is deleted.

***implementation reqs***

- :RoomList: is written to disk atomically: to a temporary file in the same folder that then replaces the previous file.

- When an application does not apply a requested window frame, :RoomsApp: requests it once more after 100 ms and then accepts the frame the application settled on.

- :Implementation: is written in Swift, Swift 5 language mode, built with Swift Package Manager (swift-tools-version 5.10), for macOS 14 or newer on Apple silicon and Intel.

- :Implementation: is one Swift package with a `Package.swift` at the build folder root and three products: the library target `RoomsCore` (Foundation only, no AppKit), the library target `RoomsKit` (depends on `RoomsCore`, links AppKit, SwiftUI, ApplicationServices, Carbon), and the executable target `Rooms` whose `main.swift` only starts the app from `RoomsKit`.

- :Implementation: has no Xcode project, no `.app` bundle, no App Sandbox, and no code signing; `swift build` and `swift run Rooms` are the only build and launch commands.

- :RoomsApp: uses AppKit: `NSApplication` with the `.accessory` activation policy, `NSStatusItem` for :MenuBarItem:, a non-activating `NSPanel` for :Palette:, and `NSWindow` for :WindowPicker: and :GettingStarted:; view content is SwiftUI hosted in `NSHostingView`.

- `PaletteViewModel.panelHeight` is the height of :Palette: computed from the :PaletteDesign: tokens and the current rows; the :Palette: panel sets its frame height to exactly this value on open and whenever it changes, and never measures `NSHostingView.fittingSize` or applies a minimum height.

- The :Palette: panel is borderless, non-opaque, with a clear background; its SwiftUI content fills the panel exactly and draws the rounded shape, fill, and border itself, and the panel shadow is invalidated after every resize so it follows the rounded shape.

- `AppController` publishes the latest :Notification: as `notification` (a `RoomsNotification` with `message` and `applications`); `NotificationDesign` in `RoomsKit` holds its checkable :NotificationDesign: values.

- :Notification: is a borderless, non-activating `NSPanel` with SwiftUI content in `NSHostingView`, at window level `.popUpMenu`, with the collection behavior `.canJoinAllSpaces`, `.fullScreenAuxiliary`, `.stationary`, and `.ignoresCycle`, ignoring mouse events and never becoming key.

- :WindowCatalog: uses the Accessibility API (`AXUIElement`) to list, move, resize, raise, minimize, and focus windows, `NSRunningApplication` to hide and activate applications, and `CGWindowListCopyWindowInfo` for window ids and z-order.

- :WindowCatalog: finds full-screen windows through `CGWindowListCopyWindowInfo` (a window of the application at layer 0 whose bounds equal a screen's frame and that the Accessibility API does not list), because the Accessibility API lists only the windows of the current Space; it takes one out of full screen by activating the application with `NSRunningApplication` and setting the window's `AXFullScreen` attribute to false.

- :WindowCatalog: finds the applications with a window on another desktop through `CGWindowListCopyWindowInfo`: a window of the application at layer 0, at least 100 by 100 points, not on the current Space, not in full screen, and not listed by the Accessibility API.

- `FakeWindowSystem` can put a window in full screen or on another desktop; such a window is not listed, like the Accessibility API.

- :AppWindow: identity is the application bundle identifier, the process identifier, and the `CGWindowID`; :WindowCatalog: pairs an Accessibility window element with its `CGWindowID` through the private function `_AXUIElementGetWindow`.

- :WindowPicker: snapshots are taken with `CGWindowListCreateImage` for the window's `CGWindowID`.

- Global hotkeys (⌥Space, ⌃⌥1–9, ⌃⌥ arrows, ⌃⌥↩) are registered with the Carbon `RegisterEventHotKey` API; keys inside :Palette: and :WindowPicker: are handled by the panel or window itself.

- :RoomList: is stored as one JSON file at `~/Library/Application Support/Rooms/rooms.json`, encoded with `Codable`, dates in ISO 8601, and loaded at launch; a missing or unreadable file is treated as an empty :RoomList: and the unreadable file is kept as `rooms.json.corrupt`.

- The first-launch flag for :GettingStarted: is stored in `UserDefaults` under the key `hasLaunchedBefore`.

- `RoomsCore` contains the pure logic as separate types: `Room`, `AppWindow`, `Layout`, `LayoutEngine`, `RoomStore` (JSON persistence), `RoomMatcher` (name matching and ordering), and `WindowMatcher` (re-finding saved windows).

- `RoomsKit` defines a `WindowSystem` protocol covering every call :WindowCatalog: makes to the Accessibility API, `NSRunningApplication`, and window snapshots; `AccessibilityWindowSystem` is the real implementation, and every other type in `RoomsKit` receives a `WindowSystem` by injection.

- `RoomsKit` exposes an `AppController` facade that owns the `RoomStore`, :CurrentRoom:, the `WindowSystem`, and one view model per surface (`PaletteViewModel`, `WindowPickerViewModel`, `StatusMenuModel`, `GettingStartedModel`); every user action in :Palette:, :WindowPicker:, and the :MenuBarItem: menu is a method on one of these view models, and views only bind to them.

- Every Accessibility call that fails for one window is logged with `os.Logger` (subsystem `dev.rooms.app`) and that window is skipped; a failure never stops the rest of the operation or crashes :RoomsApp:.

- All layout arithmetic uses AppKit screen coordinates in points and integral frames, rounded to whole points.

- `RoomsKit` includes `FakeWindowSystem`, a public in-memory `WindowSystem` whose applications, windows, screens, permission flags, and recorded operations are set and read directly by test code.

- `FakeWindowSystem` can give a window a resize delay in milliseconds, applied on its fake clock advanced by `wait(milliseconds:)`, and can make a window ignore resize requests.

- A stored :Layout: value `myLayout` from an earlier version of :RoomsApp: loads as Auto, and stored `myLayoutFrames` are ignored.

- `RoomStore` and `AppController` take the JSON file location by injection, defaulting to the Application Support path.

- `Package.swift` declares the test targets `RoomsCoreTests` (depends on `RoomsCore`), `RoomsKitTests` (depends on `RoomsKit`), and `ConformanceTests` at `Tests/ConformanceTests` (depends on `RoomsCore` and `RoomsKit`), the last holding only an empty `Placeholder.swift` with no test cases.

- :UnitTests: use XCTest and live in `Tests/RoomsCoreTests` and `Tests/RoomsKitTests`, one test file per type under test.

- :UnitTests: of `RoomsKit` drive view models and `AppController` with `FakeWindowSystem` and a `RoomStore` in a temporary directory; they never call the Accessibility API, open a window, register a hotkey, or touch the user's Application Support folder.

- :UnitTests: are executed via the run script [test_scripts/run_unittests_swift.sh](test_scripts/run_unittests_swift.sh).

***test reqs***

- :ConformanceTests: are XCTest test cases in Swift, one or more `.swift` files per functional spec, compiled into the package's `ConformanceTests` target.

- :ConformanceTests: `@testable import RoomsCore` and `@testable import RoomsKit`, and drive the app only through `AppController` and its view models with a `FakeWindowSystem`; they never call the Accessibility API, open a window, register a hotkey, or need a macOS permission.

- :ConformanceTests: give `AppController` a `RoomStore` in a fresh temporary directory per test.

- :ConformanceTests: must be implemented and executed; no test is skipped.

- :ConformanceTests: are prepared via the prepare script [test_scripts/prepare_environment_swift.sh](test_scripts/prepare_environment_swift.sh).

- :ConformanceTests: are executed via the run script [test_scripts/run_conformance_tests_swift.sh](test_scripts/run_conformance_tests_swift.sh).

***functional specs***

- :RoomsApp: runs as a menu bar application and shows :MenuBarItem:.
  - The menu has, in order: a disabled header with the name of :CurrentRoom: or "No room", Show Palette (⌥Space), Edit Windows…, Rename…, Getting Started, Quit Rooms.
  - Edit Windows… and Rename… are disabled when there is no :CurrentRoom:.
  - Quit Rooms quits :RoomsApp:.

- Pressing ⌥Space anywhere toggles :Palette:.
  - :Palette: opens on :CurrentScreen:, above all other windows, with its text field focused and empty.
  - :Palette: also opens from Show Palette in the :MenuBarItem: menu.
  - Esc, ⌥Space, or a click outside :Palette: closes it.
  - The bottom of :Palette: shows the key hints "↵ Go" and "esc Close".

- :Palette: lists every :Room: in :RoomList: below the text field, most recently shown first, then rooms never shown in creation order.
  - A row shows the application icons of the room's windows, the name, and "<layout name> · <count> windows".
  - The right end of a row shows "Current" when the room is :CurrentRoom:, otherwise its direct key as "⌃⌥<n>" when set.
  - The first row is selected when :Palette: opens; ↑ and ↓ move the selection.
  - When :RoomList: is empty and the text field is empty, the list area shows "No rooms yet. Type a name and press ↵ to create one."

- Typing in the :Palette: text field filters the room list to the :Room: items whose name matches the typed text, and selects the first match.
  - A name matches when every typed character appears in the name in the typed order, not necessarily adjacent, ignoring case.
  - Matches are ordered: names starting with the typed text first, then names where the typed text starts a word, then the rest; ties keep the unfiltered order.
  - When the typed text is not empty and is not exactly an existing name, ignoring case, a last row "Create “<typed text>”" follows the matches; it is selected when there are no matches.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - Typing `de` over the rooms Design, Deep Work, and Daily Build lists Design then Deep Work, then the row "Create “de”".
    - Neither Design nor Deep Work was shown before, and Design was created first.
    - Daily Build is not listed.
    - Design is selected.

- :WindowCatalog: lists the open :AppWindow: items of every running application.
  - Only standard windows are listed; menus, tooltips, utility panels, and the windows of :RoomsApp: itself are not.
  - Minimized windows and windows of hidden applications are listed.
  - When :RoomsApp: lacks the macOS Accessibility permission, listing is not possible and :RoomsApp: shows a dialog "Rooms needs Accessibility access to see and move windows" with the buttons Open System Settings and Cancel; Open System Settings opens the Accessibility pane of System Settings.

- Pressing ↵ on the Create row of :Palette: closes :Palette: and opens :WindowPicker: for a new :Room: with the typed name, centered on :CurrentScreen:.
  - The title reads "Choose the windows for “<name>”".
  - There is one card per :AppWindow: from :WindowCatalog:, in the order listed, showing a snapshot image of the window, the application icon in a corner, and the window title below it, or the application name when the title is empty.
  - Without the macOS Screen Recording permission, the card shows the application icon in place of the snapshot.
  - The buttons Cancel and Create Room sit below the cards; Cancel closes :WindowPicker: without saving.

- Clicking a card in :WindowPicker: toggles its selection.
  - A selected card shows a highlighted border and a badge with its place number: 1 for the first selected card, then in selection order.
  - Deselecting a card removes its badge and renumbers the remaining selected cards to close the gap.
  - Create Room is enabled only while at least one card is selected.

- :WindowCatalog: measures the minimum size of an :AppWindow:.
  - It asks the window to resize to 1 by 1 point, reads the size the application actually allows, and restores the original frame.
  - Applications apply a resize with a delay, so the size is read every 50 ms until it differs from the original size and two reads in a row match, or 500 ms have passed, and the last read is the minimum size.
  - A width or height the application did not shrink at all is recorded as 0.
  - The window is not moved, and its content is not changed.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - An 800 by 600 window whose application applies resizes 120 ms after the request and allows at least 400 by 300 is measured as 400 by 300, and ends at its original frame.
  - An 800 by 600 window whose application ignores resize requests is measured as 0 by 0, and ends at its original frame.

- Create Room in :WindowPicker: saves a :Room:.
  - The windows are the selected cards in place-number order; the :Layout: is Auto; the direct key is the lowest one from 1 to 9 that no other :Room: has, or none when all nine are taken.
  - :WindowCatalog: measures the minimum size of each selected :AppWindow: before saving.
  - The :Room: is appended to :RoomList: and :RoomList: is written to disk, so it is present after :RoomsApp: relaunches.
  - :WindowPicker: closes.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - Selecting cards B, A, then C and pressing Create Room saves the room with windows in the order B, A, C.
    - The saved :Room: has :Layout: Auto and the lowest free direct key.
    - Each saved :AppWindow: carries the minimum size :WindowCatalog: measured.
    - A :RoomList: loaded again from the same file contains that :Room: with the same window order.

- :WindowCatalog: finds a saved :AppWindow: again among the open windows.
  - It matches the window identity first; failing that, a window of the same application with the same title; failing that, the first window of the same application not matched by another saved :AppWindow: of the same room.
  - An :AppWindow: whose application is not running is not found.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - A saved window whose application was relaunched with new identities is found by its title.
    - Two saved windows of the same application whose titles both changed are matched to that application's remaining windows in saved order, each window at most once.

- :LayoutEngine: computes a frame for each :AppWindow: of an ordered list for one of Focus, Columns, Grid, or Stack within the visible area of :CurrentScreen:.
  - Frames follow the :Layout: definition, with an 8-point gap between frames and between a frame and the area edge.
  - Focus with a single window fills the area.
  - Stack gives the first window the whole area, and each later window the previous frame moved 32 points right and 32 points down and shrunk by 32 points in width and height.

- :LayoutEngine: adapts the frames of Focus, Columns, Grid, and Stack to the minimum size of each :AppWindow:.
  - A window whose frame would be smaller than its minimum size gets its minimum size, and the other windows in the same row or column share the space that is left, keeping the 8-point gaps.
  - When the minimum sizes together are larger than the area, the frames overlap by equal amounts and every frame stays inside the area.
  - A frame is never larger than the area.
  - A :Layout: fits when its adapted frames do not overlap; Stack always fits.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - Three windows with minimum widths 700, 100, and 100 on a 1440 by 900 visible area get Columns widths 700, 354, and 354, side by side with 8-point gaps.
  - Three windows with minimum width 600 on a 1440 by 900 visible area get Columns frames 600 wide that overlap by equal amounts, the first starting at the left edge and the last ending at the right edge, each 8 points inside.

- :LayoutEngine: resolves Auto to the first of Focus, Columns, Grid that fits, in that order, and to Stack when none fits.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - Three windows with minimum width 400 on a 1440 by 900 visible area resolve Auto to Focus.
  - Three windows with minimum width 1000 on a 1440 by 900 visible area resolve Auto to Stack.
    - Focus, Columns, and Grid each leave at least one frame narrower than 1000.

- Pressing ↵ on a room row in :Palette:, or clicking the row, shows the :Room:.
  - :Palette: closes.
  - :WindowCatalog: finds each :AppWindow: of the room.
  - When a window is not found and its application has a window in full screen, that window is taken out of full screen first; :RoomsApp: waits until it is listed again, at most 2 seconds, and then finds the room's windows again.
  - :LayoutEngine: computes frames on :CurrentScreen: for the found windows in room order using the room's :Layout:, resolving Auto as defined.
  - Each found window is unminimized, moved to its frame, and raised; the main window is raised last and gets keyboard focus.
  - When any :AppWindow: of the room is not found, because its application quit or its window was closed, nothing moves and a notification "Open <application names>, then open “<name>” again" is shown.
  - The names are those of the applications of the windows not found, each once in room order, joined with ", " and a final " and ".
  - When the application of every window not found has a window on another desktop, the notification is instead "<application names> is on another desktop. Move it to this one, then open “<name>” again", with "are" and "them" for more than one application; nothing moves.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - Showing a Focus room with three found windows on a 1440 by 900 visible area places window 1 at the left two-thirds frame and windows 2 and 3 stacked in the right third.
    - Window 2 was minimized before and is unminimized.
    - Window 1 is raised last and has keyboard focus.
  - Showing a room R whose windows belong to applications A, B, and C, in that order, after A quit moves no window and shows "Open A, then open “R” again".
    - With A and B quit, the notification is "Open A and B, then open “R” again".
  - Showing R after the window of A was closed while A keeps running moves no window and shows "Open A, then open “R” again".
  - Showing R while the window of C is on another desktop moves no window and shows "C is on another desktop. Move it to this one, then open “R” again".
    - With the windows of B and C on another desktop, the notification is "B and C are on another desktop. Move them to this one, then open “R” again".
  - Showing R on Columns while the window of A is in full screen takes that window out of full screen and moves it to its Columns frame, with no notification.

- Showing a :Room: hides every window that is not in the room.
  - Every running application with no found window in the room is hidden.
  - Every other window of an application that has a found window in the room is minimized.
  - No window is closed.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - Showing room A (windows 1 and 2 of application X) while application Y and a third window of X are open hides application Y and minimizes window 3.
    - Showing room B (window 3 of X and window 4 of Y) afterwards unhides Y, unminimizes windows 3 and 4, and minimizes windows 1 and 2.
    - After both operations the open window count is unchanged.

- Showing a :Room: makes it :CurrentRoom:, sets its Last shown to now, and writes :RoomList: to disk.
  - The header of the :MenuBarItem: menu shows the new :CurrentRoom: name.

- Create Room in :WindowPicker: shows the new :Room: right after saving it.

- Pressing ⇥ in :Palette: sets the selected room's :Layout: to the next one in the cycle, and ⇧⇥ to the previous one.
  - The cycle order is Auto, Focus, Columns, Grid, Stack; every layout is offered whether or not it fits the room's windows.
  - The change is written to :RoomList: at once.
  - When the selected room is :CurrentRoom:, its found windows move to the new frames at once.
  - The bottom of :Palette: shows "Here: <resolved layout name>" for the selected room and the key hint "⇥ Layout".

  ***acceptance tests***
  - For a room on Auto with three windows, pressing ⇥ five times yields Focus, Columns, Grid, Stack, Auto in that order, and ⇧⇥ then yields Stack.
    - After each press the room's :Layout: in a :RoomList: reloaded from disk is the one shown.
  - For a room on Auto with three windows of minimum width 1000 on a 1440 by 900 visible area, pressing ⇥ four times still yields Focus, Columns, Grid, Stack.

- When :RoomsApp: moves the windows of a :Room: to the frames of a :Layout: that :LayoutEngine: had to adapt, a :Notification: says so.
  - It applies when a :Room: is shown and when ⇥ or ⇧⇥ moves the windows of :CurrentRoom:.
  - Before adapting, each window whose saved minimum size is larger than its unadapted frame is asked to take the unadapted frame, of Focus when the :Layout: is Auto; the size it keeps becomes its minimum size, which can be smaller than the saved one.
  - After moving the windows, a window that stayed wider or taller than its frame gets the width or height it kept as its minimum size, and the frames are adapted and applied once more before the :Notification: is chosen.
  - :WindowCatalog: reads a window's frame after it settles: every 50 ms until it has the requested frame, or it has changed and two reads in a row match, at most 500 ms.
  - A changed minimum size is written to :RoomList:.
  - The applications are those of the windows whose minimum size is larger than their unadapted frame, each once in room order.
  - When the adapted frames do not overlap, the message is "<application names> can't get smaller, so <layout name> was adjusted to fit".
  - When they overlap, the message is "<application names> can't get small enough for <layout name> here, so some windows overlap".
  - When Auto resolves to Stack because none of Focus, Columns, Grid fits, the message is "The windows of “<name>” are too large for Focus, Columns, or Grid here, so Stack is used".
  - Nothing is shown when no frame was adapted.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - Showing a Columns room R whose windows belong to applications A, B, and C, where the window of A has minimum width 700, on a 1440 by 900 visible area shows "A can't get smaller, so Columns was adjusted to fit".
  - With every window of R at minimum width 600, the notification is "A, B and C can't get small enough for Columns here, so some windows overlap".
  - Showing R on Auto with every window at minimum width 1000 shows "The windows of “R” are too large for Focus, Columns, or Grid here, so Stack is used".
  - Showing a Grid room of four windows on a 1440 by 900 visible area, where the window of A has a saved minimum size of 0 by 0 but never gets smaller than 900 by 600, ends with A at 900 by 600 in the top-left cell and the other three windows adapted around it without overlap.
    - The saved minimum size of A in a :RoomList: reloaded from disk is 900 by 600, and the notification is "A can't get smaller, so Grid was adjusted to fit".
  - Showing a Focus room of three windows on a 1440 by 900 visible area, where windows 2 and 3 have a saved minimum height of 884 but can get as small as 300 by 200, stacks windows 2 and 3 in the right third without overlap and shows no notification.
    - Their saved minimum heights in a :RoomList: reloaded from disk are no larger than their frames.

- While :Palette: is open, pressing ⇥ or ⇧⇥ shows the layout preview: a click-through overlay covering the visible area of :CurrentScreen:, above every window except :Palette:, drawn per :LayoutPreviewDesign:.
  - The overlay has one card per saved window of the selected :Room:, at the frame :LayoutEngine: computes for the room's :Layout: on :CurrentScreen:, showing the application icon, the application name, and the window title.
  - When the layout changes, the cards move to their new frames over 200 ms.
  - ↑ and ↓ switch the cards to the newly selected room.
  - The overlay stays visible when the selected room is :CurrentRoom: and its windows moved.
  - The overlay hides when the Create row is selected and when :Palette: closes.

- Pressing ⌃⌥← or ⌃⌥→ moves the focused window to the left or right half of the visible area of :CurrentScreen:, ⌃⌥↑ or ⌃⌥↓ to the top or bottom half, and ⌃⌥↩ to the whole area.
  - Frames keep an 8-point gap from the area edges and from the middle.
  - Pressing the same ⌃⌥← or ⌃⌥→ again while the window is in that half changes it to that side's third, then two thirds, then back to the half.

- Pressing ⌘S in :Palette: saves the windows visible on :CurrentScreen: as a new :Room: named "Room <n>", with the smallest n of 1 or more that no other :Room: uses, ignoring case.
  - The windows are those :WindowCatalog: lists on :CurrentScreen: that are not minimized and whose application is not hidden, front to back; the frontmost is place 1.
  - :WindowCatalog: measures the minimum size of each window.
  - The :Layout: is the first of Focus, Columns, Grid, Stack whose computed frames are within 24 points on each edge of the windows' current frames, and Auto otherwise; no window is moved.
  - The :Room: gets the lowest direct key from 1 to 9 that no other :Room: has, or none when all nine are taken.
  - The :Room: is appended to :RoomList:, :RoomList: is written, the :Room: becomes :CurrentRoom:, and its row is selected in :Palette:, which stays open.
  - When no window is visible on :CurrentScreen:, nothing is saved.
  - The bottom of :Palette: shows the key hint "⌘S New room"; for 2 seconds after ⌘S it reads "Saved “Room <n>”" instead.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - With rooms "Room 1" and "room 3" in :RoomList: and three visible windows within 24 points of their Columns frames, ⌘S saves "Room 2" with :Layout: Columns and those three windows front to back.
    - "Room 2" is :CurrentRoom:, its row is selected, the hint reads "Saved “Room 2”", and it is in a :RoomList: reloaded from disk.
  - Two visible windows in no tidy arrangement are saved as a new :Room: on Auto, and neither window moved.
  - A minimized window and a window of a hidden application are not part of the new :Room:.

- Right-clicking a room row in :Palette: opens a context menu with Edit Windows…, Rename…, and Delete.
  - The selected row also shows "↵" and an ⓧ button at its right end, after the direct key or "Current".

- Deleting a :Room: with the ⓧ on its row, Delete in its context menu, or ⌘⌫ on the selected row removes it from :RoomList: and writes :RoomList: to disk.
  - When it was :CurrentRoom:, :CurrentRoom: becomes none.
  - No window is moved, hidden, or closed.
  - The row below it, or the last row, becomes selected.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - Deleting the :CurrentRoom: with ⌘⌫ leaves :CurrentRoom: as none, the :MenuBarItem: header reads "No room", and no window in :WindowCatalog: was moved, hidden, minimized, or closed.
    - Deleting the last row selects the new last row.

- Rename… in a row's context menu, or in the :MenuBarItem: menu for :CurrentRoom:, opens a dialog "Rename “<name>”" with a text field holding the name and the buttons Cancel and Rename.
  - Rename is enabled only when the trimmed text is not empty and no other :Room: has that name, ignoring case; otherwise the dialog shows "A room with this name already exists".
  - Rename sets the name and writes :RoomList: to disk; ↵ triggers Rename and Esc triggers Cancel.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - Renaming Design to `deep work` while Deep Work exists disables Rename and shows "A room with this name already exists"; renaming it to `Design 2` succeeds and the new name is in a :RoomList: reloaded from disk.

- Edit Windows… in a row's context menu, or in the :MenuBarItem: menu for :CurrentRoom:, opens :WindowPicker: for that room, titled "Edit the windows of “<name>”", with the button Save Room in place of Create Room.
  - The cards of the room's found windows start selected, numbered in room order; a saved window that is not found is left out.
  - Clicking a selected card removes it from the selection; clicking it again appends it at the end.
  - Save Room replaces the room's windows with the selected cards in place-number order, measures their minimum sizes again, writes :RoomList: to disk, and closes :WindowPicker:.
  - When the room is :CurrentRoom:, it is shown again after saving.

- When :RoomList: is loaded, each :Room: without a direct key gets the lowest one from 1 to 9 that no other :Room: has, in creation order, and :RoomList: is written.
  - A :Room: beyond the ninth keeps no direct key; when a :Room: is deleted, its key goes to the first :Room: without one, in creation order.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - Loading rooms A, B, C, and D, created in that order, where only C has key 1, gives A key 2, B key 3, and D key 4.
  - With ten rooms, the tenth has no key; deleting the room with key 5 gives key 5 to the tenth.

- Pressing ⌘1 to ⌘9 in :Palette: gives the selected room that direct key and writes :RoomList: to disk.
  - The :Room: that had that key takes the selected room's previous key, or none when the selected room had none.
  - Pressing the key the selected room already has changes nothing.
  - The bottom of :Palette: shows the key hint "⌘1–9 Key".

  ***acceptance tests***
  - With room A on key 1 and room B on key 2, pressing ⌘2 on A gives A key 2 and B key 1; pressing ⌘2 on A again keeps both keys.
    - Each state is present in a :RoomList: reloaded from disk.

- Pressing ⌃⌥1 to ⌃⌥9 anywhere shows the :Room: with that direct key, as if chosen in :Palette:.
  - When no :Room: has that key, nothing happens.

- :GettingStarted: opens from Getting Started in the :MenuBarItem: menu, and on the first launch of :RoomsApp:.
  - It is a window centered on :CurrentScreen: with the title "Getting Started" and four numbered steps: 1 Open the windows a project needs. 2 Press ⌥Space, type a name for the room, and press ↵. 3 Click the windows that belong in it; the number on a card is its place, and 1 is the main window. Then Create Room. 4 Press ⇥ in ⌥Space to change the layout.
  - Below the steps: "From then on, ⌥Space and the room's name, or ⌃⌥1–9, brings it back." and a Done button that closes the window.

- The key hints at the bottom of :Palette: form one row: "Here: <resolved layout name>", "⇥ Layout", "⌘S New room", and "⌘1–9 Key" on the left, and "↵ Go" and "esc Close" on the right in a lighter color.
  - "Here: <resolved layout name>" is omitted when no room is selected.
  - "⇥ Layout" shows the tab symbol as an arrow to a bar.

- :Palette:, :WindowPicker:, :GettingStarted:, :Notification:, and the dialogs of :RoomsApp: use the light appearance regardless of the system appearance.

- :Palette: is drawn per :PaletteDesign:.
  - :Palette: is 640 points wide with a 22-point corner radius, and each row is 64 points high.
  - The selected row has a solid fill in the selection color of :PaletteDesign: and white text; its right end shows the row's trailer, then "↵", then the ⓧ button.
  - Nothing is drawn between the list and the key hints.
  - The height of :Palette: is the sum of the search row, divider, list, and key hints heights of :PaletteDesign:; nothing is cut off, and all four corners show the 22-point radius.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - With one :Room: in :RoomList: and an empty text field, `PaletteViewModel.panelHeight` is 213.
  - With three :Room: items and an empty text field, `PaletteViewModel.panelHeight` is 349, and with nine it is 757.
  - With one :Room: named "alpha", typing "zz" leaves only the Create row, and `PaletteViewModel.panelHeight` is 213; typing "a" then shows "alpha" and the Create row, and it is 281.
  - With an empty :RoomList: and an empty text field, `PaletteViewModel.panelHeight` is 213.

- :Notification: is drawn per :NotificationDesign:, above every other window of :RoomsApp: and of other applications, on every Space and over full-screen applications.
  - The notifications about windows not found list the applications of those windows, and show their icons before the message.
  - It is centered at the top of the visible area of :CurrentScreen:, stays 4 seconds, and a new :Notification: replaces the one showing.
  - It never takes keyboard focus, and clicks pass through it.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - Showing a room R whose windows belong to applications A, B, and C, in that order, with A and B quit publishes a :Notification: with the message "Open A and B, then open “R” again" and the applications A and B, in that order.
  - `NotificationDesign` has the fill #FAFAFC, the corner radius 16, the accent #3B76F6, and the duration 4 seconds.
